    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID47",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "answer_relevance_card_7bf4a7dec97d54b11e32ea5a__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>1.2 incitement and encouragement of harm – Inciting or encouraging an individual, group, or general audience to harm a woman or group of women. It includes language where the author seeks to rationalise and/or justify harming women to another person.</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Online communities, including incel and men’s rights activist groups, promote hatred of women, and members of these groups have committed real-world violence against women. The internet provides spaces for misogynistic ideas to proliferate, including the celebration of a perpetrator of violence as a hero among incels. Perpetrators associated with incels have framed harm against women as retribution for denied sexual access, grounding their justification in a perceived entitlement to sex.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
