        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>3.1 casual use of gendered slurs, profanities, and insults – Using gendered slurs, gender-based profanities and insults, but not to intentionally attack women. Only terms that traditionally describe women are in scope (e.g. ‘b*tch’, ‘sl*t’).</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender-biased language implicitly or explicitly favours one gender over another and constitutes gender discrimination, while sexist language is defined by intentional derogatory and discriminatory use against a particular gender. Gender-neutral language refers to people without specifying women or men, and gender-inclusive language places women and men on equal footing without conveying gender stereotypes.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
