    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID94",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_6d5835235331eee3dea75ecd__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Illustrative Gender Stereotypes and Gender Discriminatory Roles\n\nMen as tough, unfeeling, aggressive, and expected to conceal or suppress emotional vulnerability - ‘stiff upper lip’ culture.\n\nMen as dominant in the family, within intimate relationships and in social settings with women.\n\nMen as the sexual initiator or entitled to sex with women. Some groups of men in minority groups as more sexually violent and more controlling (based for example on income levels, regions, ethnicity, and/ or religious affiliation).\n\nCulture of acceptance or victim-blaming around rape, especially framed in the light that if the survivor 'had been drinking alcohol', 'wearing clothes considered provocative', 'walking home alone at night', or was ‘leading’ the perpetrator ‘on’.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes portray men as tough, unfeeling, and aggressive, expect them to suppress emotional vulnerability, and assign them dominance within families, intimate relationships, and social settings with women. They also cast men as sexual initiators or entitled to sex with women, while stereotyping some minority groups of men as more sexually violent and controlling based on income, region, ethnicity, or religious affiliation. A culture of rape acceptance or victim-blaming frames survivors’ drinking, clothing, independent movement at night, or perceived sexual encouragement as grounds for blame.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_6d5835235331eee3dea75ecd__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
