      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>3.2 immutable gender differences and gender stereotypes – Asserting immutable, natural or otherwise essential differences between men and women. In some cases, this could be in the form of using women’s traits to attack men. Most sexist jokes will fall into this category.</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes portray men as tough, unfeeling, and aggressive, expect them to suppress emotional vulnerability, and assign them dominance within families, intimate relationships, and social settings with women. They also cast men as sexual initiators or entitled to sex with women, while stereotyping some minority groups of men as more sexually violent and controlling based on income, region, ethnicity, or religious affiliation. A culture of rape acceptance or victim-blaming frames survivors’ drinking, clothing, independent movement at night, or perceived sexual encouragement as grounds for blame.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
