    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID146",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_7aea2367a5a9a3576272d19e__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Illustrative Gender Stereotypes and Gender Discriminatory Roles\n\nMen’s social standing and reputation as upheld by the loyalty, obedience and fidelity of their female spouses, siblings and children, the absence of which may be a justification for violence.\n\nMen considered as effeminate and weak if they are gender egalitarian or support feminist movements.\n\nNotions of femininity based on passivity, vulnerability, purity and heterosexuality.\n\nWomen who assert, speak out, or defend equal rights as ‘man-hating’ or unwomanly.\n\nLesbian, bisexual, and transgender women as outside the feminine ‘norm’ and as needing policing/ correction.\n\nSome groups of minority women as inherently more sexually available or “up for it”.\n\nFamilies and elders as entitled to control girls’ and women’s behaviour more rigidly than boys’/men’s, and to make decisions about marriage, dating, and other aspects of women and girls’ social lives.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes justify violence through expectations that women’s loyalty, obedience, and fidelity uphold men’s social standing, with their absence treated as justification for violence. Femininity is associated with passivity, vulnerability, purity, and heterosexuality, while women who assert or defend equal rights are labeled ‘man-hating’ or unwomanly, and men who support gender equality are considered effeminate and weak. Lesbian, bisexual, and transgender women are portrayed as outside the feminine norm and as needing policing or correction, some minority women are portrayed as inherently more sexually available, and families and elders are treated as entitled to control girls’ and women’s behavior more rigidly than boys’ and men’s, including decisions about marriage and dating.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_7aea2367a5a9a3576272d19e__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
