      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>4.2 supporting systemic discrimination against women as a group – Expressing support for systemic discrimination of women as a group. Support can be shown by denying, understating, or seeking to justify such discrimination.</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes justify violence through expectations that women’s loyalty, obedience, and fidelity uphold men’s social standing, with their absence treated as justification for violence. Femininity is associated with passivity, vulnerability, purity, and heterosexuality, while women who assert or defend equal rights are labeled ‘man-hating’ or unwomanly, and men who support gender equality are considered effeminate and weak. Lesbian, bisexual, and transgender women are portrayed as outside the feminine norm and as needing policing or correction, some minority women are portrayed as inherently more sexually available, and families and elders are treated as entitled to control girls’ and women’s behavior more rigidly than boys’ and men’s, including decisions about marriage and dating.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
